Welcome to tablet 2! The grammar is going to get more complicated this time,
but if you made it through tablet 1, you should be well-prepared for what comes
next.

In particular, nouns will now appear in two additional cases: the "genitive"
for one noun modifying another, and the "ablative" for the source of a movement
or the tool used for an action. Verbs will sometimes describe what other people
are doing instead of just you yourself. And some words will be written with
"logograms": special cuneiform signs that represent whole words, like how "7"
is short for "seven". When this happens, the names of those cuneiform signs
will be written in CAPITAL LETTERS separated by dots.

Don't worry, though; you can still click any word to see its definition, and
the basic vocabulary isn't going anywhere. Good luck! You've got this!

> askan sarrahhun
This line could be interpreted in multiple ways:
    1: Nu parnaš āškan šarraḫḫun.
    2: Nu šakuniyaš āškan šarraḫḫun.
It seems the intended interpretation is number...

